================================================================================
  WP3 -- NELDER-MEAD SIMPLEX vs BRUTE-FORCE GRID SEARCH
  run_04_nelder_mead.py  |  hand-implemented, penalty-constrained
================================================================================

Run settings
--------------------
  replications per policy                100 
  base seed                              20250402 
  worker processes                       14 
  horizon                                10080 min  (1 operational week)
  quick mode                             off 
  reps per evaluation                    50 
  penalty mu                             500.0 
  stopping xtol / ftol                   0.05 / 0.02 

STEP 1  The optimisation problem
------------------------------------
  decision variables                     k1 (Level III slack), k2 (Level IV slack) 
  objective                              minimise W_total(k1,k2) -- mean waiting time per patient 
  constraint                             SL_3 >= 95%,  SL_4 >= 95%   [paper Eq. 32] 
  penalised form                         F = W_total + 500 * sum_l max(0, SL_min - SL_l) 
  box bounds                             k1 in [0, 30], k2 in [0, 120] 
  noise handling                         sample average over 50 fixed CRN streams 

  Bounds are enforced by PROJECTION (each trial vertex is clipped back into the box) rather than by a second penalty term, so the simplex can slide along a bound instead of being pushed away from it.


STEP 2  Single-start Nelder-Mead (live iteration trace)
-----------------------------------------------------------
  initial vertex x0                      (10, 30) 
  initial simplex step                   (4, 15) 
  A per-dimension step is used because k1 and k2 live on different scales (k1 in ~[0,30] against T_3 = 30, k2 in ~[0,120] against T_4 = 120); one scalar step would make the initial simplex badly conditioned.

