  IFP vs GCMU             +6.822           +8.378   216.1898     8.5023            96.07%       25.4x           2.9175           0.5786                  3.9
  ALT vs SBP              -2.371           -1.696   220.7672     2.1457            99.03%      102.9x           2.9482           0.2907                  1.0
  ALT vs WSEPT            +0.219           +0.066   191.6437     0.1071            99.94%     1789.3x           2.7469           0.0649                  0.1
  ALT vs GCMU             -0.835           -1.664   223.7516     0.4610            99.79%      485.4x           2.9681           0.1347                  0.2
  SBP vs WSEPT            +2.590           +1.762   198.0362     2.2323            98.87%       88.7x           2.7923           0.2965                  1.1
  SBP vs GCMU             +1.536           +0.032   268.9685     1.0273            99.62%      261.8x           3.2542           0.2011                  0.4
  WSEPT vs GCMU           -1.055           -1.730   215.2730     0.5935            99.72%      362.7x           2.9113           0.1529                  0.3

  variance removed (median across pairs) 98.95% 
  efficiency gain (median across pairs)  95.8x 
  efficiency gain (range)                19.4x .. 1789.3x 

  [OK] On variance alone, one CRN replication carries about as much comparative information as 96 independent ones (99.0% of the difference's variance removed).
  Read as a saving, 100 independent replications are matched by roughly 1.0 CRN replications -- but that is an upper bound on the benefit, not a recommendation. The efficiency ratio is itself estimated from these data, and a t-interval needs enough degrees of freedom to be trustworthy. The practical reading is that 20-30 CRN replications give comparisons at least as sharp as the paper's 100 independent ones -- a 3-5x saving that is safe to actually take.


