3.4.3  Robustness to the service-time distribution
------------------------------------------------------
  Replacing TruncExp with a truncated lognormal calibrated to the same nominal means (sigma = 0.3). The lognormal has a heavier right tail, which queueing theory says should raise congestion; the question is whether it changes the RANKING.

  truncexp                   [##################################]   500/500   100.0%    5.6s elapsed,   0.0s left  
  trunclognormal             [##################################]   500/500   100.0%    7.4s elapsed,   0.0s left  
  distribution comparison: 13.0 s

  Effect of swapping the consultation-time distribution
  policy  TruncExp wait  Lognormal wait  change  relative  SL4 % (exp)  SL4 % (lognormal)
  ------  -------------  --------------  ------  --------  -----------  -----------------
  IFP             52.43           75.43  +23.00    +43.9%       100.00             100.00
  ALT             42.39           64.42  +22.04    +52.0%        87.37              76.65
  SBP             44.08           67.70  +23.62    +53.6%        89.40              80.57
  WSEPT           42.32           64.46  +22.14    +52.3%        86.87              75.69
  GCMU            44.05           66.42  +22.37    +50.8%        90.99              82.48

  ranking under TruncExp                 WSEPT < ALT < GCMU < SBP < IFP 
  ranking under truncated lognormal      ALT < WSEPT < GCMU < SBP < IFP 

  [!!] The ranking changed between the two service-time distributions. Any conclusion about which policy is best must therefore be stated together with the distributional assumption it rests on.


